Z||000000||N_Man||04-20-2000||01:13 AM||||OK I've been looking through past posts and wanted to confirm a couple of things.<P>If I get a 3.1 engine with cast iron heads I can take the bottom end of the engine and bolt on my heads, intake, etc.<BR>If I understood correctly I CAN do this and have a working running 3.1 with no modification.<P>If anybody has any advice, or has done this to theirs please help<P>BTW the other thread was <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000192.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000192.html</A><p>[This message has been edited by N_Man (edited 04-20-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-20-2000||06:50 AM||||If you have a pre 88 Fiero you need the flywheel/flex plate from the 3.1 ||||reg||1
Z||000002||N_Man||04-20-2000||12:27 PM||||Thanks, I forgot about the flywheel there.<P>I still wanted to confirm that the pistons would be a reasonable match for the fieros heads.<BR>Also does anybody know what kind of cpmpression ratio it would have?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||04-20-2000||01:17 PM||||I was under the impression that the 3.1 iron heads were the same as the 2.8. <BR>Anybody?||||reg||1
Z||000004||87PegasusGT||04-20-2000||01:21 PM||||Technically, I'm not sure, but supposedly, the 3.1 only pulls 140hp which is the same as the 87-88 Fiero 2.8 anyway. I imagine compression would be pretty close. You will probably have some torque gain though as it is not as much of a "rev happy" engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||04-20-2000||02:08 PM||||Unless you change the cam, there would be no improvement at all and a total waste of time on your part if you care about making more power.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||04-20-2000||03:46 PM||||What's the advantage to going to the 3.1?  It only made 120 hp in the minivans and 140 hp in everything else - yet a lot of people are going to it.  Is it more torquey?  More reliable?<P>I must just be stupid and missing something, because Ed Parks said he's so busy doing 3.1 and 3.4 (ohc) conversions, he doesn't have time to do anything else.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||04-20-2000||03:48 PM||||Sorry, I meant 3.4 ohv conversions, not 3.4 TDC.  I'd edit my previous post, but thanks to a few of you chowderheads bickering, we can't edit posts anymore.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||N_Man||04-20-2000||10:56 PM||||I was under the impression that the 3.1 was more reliable and had alot more HP POTENTIAL.<P>If I am wrong somebody please correct me before I spend money.  Oh yea a cam, headers, and a chip were all going to go in as well.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||04-21-2000||03:02 PM||||The 3.1 has a longer stroke than the 2.8 and even though the HP numbers will look similar the torque is higher in the 3.1.  This can be felt in acceleration.<P>Many people "Stroke" their 2.8 to 3.1 just to get the additional torque.  (I believe stroking the 2.8 and boring it out .030" gives you a 3.2L engine).  If you are going that route it is worth the effort to install a better cam and to port the intake and exhaust manifolds.<P>I installed a Grooms long block that I got from Ed Parks.  It is a basic 2.8 rebuild that is bored .040 over making it a 2.9.  I did this to get the 18 month unlimited mileage warranty offered on this motor.  I cleaned up everything and ported everything but the heads (that would void the warranty).  Power gain is noticable (based on the shorter distance it now takes to get to 70mph up a steep mountain pass near home).<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||87PegasusGT||04-21-2000||03:16 PM||||My 2 cents? For what its worth, getting a 3.4 will be a lot better all the way around.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||04-21-2000||08:18 PM||||I have to agree with 87PegasusGT.  If you want more torque than your 2.8 can provide than you will find more of it in the 3.4-liter engine. The 3.4-liter engine is also a bolt in like the 3.1 except you have to drill new starter holes on the other side of the block. You can easily achieve 170 HP with this engine also. That would give you 0-60 times of less than 7 seconds with a manual transmission and upper 14 to low 15 quarter mile times. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shiner||04-21-2000||10:40 PM||||If you are going to go to the trouble of tearing down and engine compleatly and rebuilding it, why not keep the same stroke, put in all high quality stuff, and raise the redline to 7000rpm?  With modifications to the bearings/crank/oiling system, the 2.8's in racecars pulled up to 8000rpm I belive.  It could be a lot of fun. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||N_Man||04-21-2000||11:42 PM||||Hey Shiner I like the way you think.  I was thinking 3.1 because of a powerband issue and I know that parts are well proven and availible.  If I could get specs on parts and mods that will have the 2.8 pull strong to 7000 RPM I would be very interested in doing that.||||reg||1
